This gives every image on your website a basic rule:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Never become wider than the container you're placed inside.\strokec2 \
Then for images that need to completely fill a specific frame, override it with:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 .some-frame \{\
  width: 300px;\
  height: 200px;\
  overflow: hidden;\
\}\
\
.some-frame img \{\
  width: 100%;\
  height: 100%;\
  object-fit: cover;\
\}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 There are three important concepts:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 FRAME\
width + height\
      \uc0\u8595 \
\uc0\u9484 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9488 \
\uc0\u9474                  \u9474 \
\uc0\u9474       IMAGE      \u9474  \u8592  width:100%; height:100%\
\uc0\u9474                  \u9474 \
\uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9496 \
      \uc0\u8593 \
overflow:hidden\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 width/height
\f1\fs24  determines the 
\f2\b frame size
\f1\b0 .\

\f3\fs26 width:100%; height:100%
\f1\fs24  tells the 
\f2\b image to use the frame's dimensions
\f1\b0 .\

\f3\fs26 object-fit
\f1\fs24  determines 
\f2\b how the image fits
\f1\b0 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 object-fit: cover;\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 fills the entire border but may crop the photo.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs26 \cf0 object-fit: contain;\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 shows the entire photo but may leave empty space.\
For your portfolio, I'd generally use 
\f4\b\fs26 \strokec2 cover
\f2\fs24 \strokec2  for photo cards
\f1\b0  and 
\f4\b\fs26 \strokec2 contain
\f2\fs24 \strokec2  for screenshots/projects where you want the entire website screenshot visible
\f1\b0 .\
Also, your interest cards are now set up correctly in principle: the card is 
\f3\fs26 150 \'d7 118px
\f1\fs24 , and the image is constrained to 
\f3\fs26 height:72px; width:100%; object-fit:cover
\f1\fs24 . So if those begin overflowing again, it's usually a syntax error elsewhere rather than the dimensions themselves.\
The 
\f2\b first thing I would do right now is fix the missing 
\f4\fs26 \strokec2 \}
\f2\fs24 \strokec2  in 
\f4\fs26 \strokec2 .work-thumb
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec2 . That is a definite error in the CSS you uploaded.\
